{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdOrderNeTop","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdOrderNeTop","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.PhiBProd","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Meromorphic.Divisor"],"declarations":1,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdOrderNeTop.phiBProd_order_ne_top_of_products","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.PhiBProdOrderNeTop.phiBProd_order_ne_top_of_products","kind":"theorem","line":37,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/PhiBProdOrderNeTop.lean#L37-L58","doc":"**`phiBProd` has finite meromorphic order given both products are locally nonzero.**\nIf the numerator and denominator products are meromorphic on `ℂ` and neither is locally\nidentically zero (`meromorphicOrderAt … ≠ ⊤`), then `meromorphicOrderAt (phiBProd b) z ≠ ⊤`\nat every `z`. This discharges input 2 of the divisor-route brick (c) for the concrete\n`Φ_b`, reducing it to non-vanishing of the two products. ","signatureChunk":0}]}